    "tool": {
      "namespace": "cron",
      "description": "Manage scheduled cron jobs defined in cron.d markdown files. Use for reminders and recurring tasks. A reminder or follow-up is work carried for the user, so it belongs to a durable record: when it serves an existing user goal, that goal is this job's `owner` (do not mint a separate tracked item for a goal's check-ins or nudges), and otherwise create or log a tracked item and set `owner` to it. A reminder cron with no owner is a routing error.\n\nFor goal-owned schedules, set `owner` to `goal:<goal-slug>`; the tool writes the validated definition under `workspace/goals/<goal-slug>/crons/<frequency>/`. Keep the job's output inside that goal's workspace: documents the user asked for go in `workspace/goals/<goal-slug>/files/`, and run logs, watermarks, snapshots, and other bookkeeping go in `workspace/goals/<goal-slug>/hidden_files/`. Write the job body so a run reports through its final message and the Goals timeline. Do not instruct a run to write a per-run report file the user did not ask for under `workspace/goals/<goal-slug>/files/`, and do not direct goal-owned output into `workspace/your_files/`.\n\nSystem cron jobs returned by `list` are runtime-managed and cannot be added, updated, disabled, or removed with this tool. Definition file paths are chosen automatically from the job id and schedule; never provide them.\n\nUse `title` for the user-facing label, such as `Weekly energy check-in`. Keep\nthe stable job id a concise kebab-case identity for file names and later\nmanagement, such as `weekly-energy-check-in`.\n\nThe `body` is injected verbatim when the job fires, so put the full reminder text there. Use `mode=task` for one-shot reminders and scheduled tasks; a one-shot reminder's tracked item closes once the reminder is delivered and nothing remains owed.\n\nBefore scheduling a new job, check `action=list` and reuse or update an existing job (by id) rather than creating overlapping jobs.\n\nUpdate the same job id when changing an existing task's timing, title, or instructions. Removing a job revokes its scoped permissions; recreating it is not a repair for a failed run. Never claim that a run did not start from a terminal failure alone. Check the recorded cause and completed steps.\n\nRecurring tasks keep the newest queued occurrence by default. An older occurrence safely parked on an unanswered approval can be cancelled before its replacement starts. Check the run's recorded status before promising that a late approval will resume it; a superseded run does not resume. One-off reminders, manual runs, retries, and explicit run-all schedules retain their existing obligations.\n\nWhen a worker reports incomplete work, explain that it will not retry automatically. Inspect the result and verify external effects, then fix the cause before considering `cron.run`; rerun only after verifying it will not duplicate completed actions.\n\nWhen the user asks whether something scheduled fired, or asserts that it did, check the job's status (`status` with the job id) before answering. If the run is in flight, say the result is on the way; never confirm a fire you have not verified. Run success and the worker-written `result_summary` do not establish chat delivery. Before treating a result as already delivered, check the current conversation for a user-visible assistant response that actually gave it.\n\nA side chat connected to an external messaging service can receive scheduled results only from a cron created or changed inside that exact chat. A manual run for a cron targeting that chat must also be started inside that exact chat; running it does not change where the cron originated. From the Main chat or another side chat, do not target it. Ask the user to open that service conversation and set up or change the schedule there. If the tool rejects the request, explain what the user needs to do next and never imply that a schedule was created or changed or that a run started.\n\n\nChoosing the schedule time:\n- User states a clock time (\"9am\", \"at 7:30\"): use it exactly and omit `flexible_time`.\n- User states only a part of day (\"every morning\", \"each night\"): pick a fitting hour, use `:00` as the placeholder minute, and set `flexible_time: true`. Example: \"every morning\" -> `time: \"09:00\", flexible_time: true`. The runtime replaces the placeholder with this device's stable non-round minute and reports the actual stored fire in the result, spreading fleet load without changing this tool schema.\n- When confirming, describe the time loosely (\"around 9 each morning\"), never the exact minute, and never promise to-the-minute delivery. A user who then asks for an exact time gets theirs.\n\nChoosing the day:\n- Day words follow the user's current local date and time, never UTC. \"Today\", \"tonight\", \"tomorrow\", and weekday names all mean what they mean on the user's clock right now.\n- Shortly after midnight, day words are traps. At 00:30, \"in the morning\" and \"tomorrow morning\" almost always mean the morning a few hours away, and \"tonight\" means the night already in progress; adding a calendar day there sets the reminder a full day late.\n- Never work out a weekday by counting in your head; read the current date from your context and anchor on it. When an ask is genuinely ambiguous, pick the nearer instant and name the day you picked so the user can correct you.\n\nAfter every add or update:\n- The result reports the resolved fire as `next_run_local` (with its weekday) and `next_run_in` (the wait). Check both against what the user asked for before you confirm anything. When it is the small hours of the user's night right now, a reminder for the coming morning or tonight should be only hours away, so a wait near a full day means you added a day: fix the schedule first, then confirm. A daytime \"tomorrow morning\" ask sitting twenty-some hours out is normal; never move it to a time already past, which would fire immediately.\n- Confirm with the day made unambiguous: name the weekday or date (\"that's Tuesday the 19th\") or the honest distance (\"in about 9 hours\"). Keep self-picked minutes loose as above.",
