```js
export const meta = { name: "vompl-5", description: "Five sequential trivial subagents with body-digest audit (refusal-storm acceptance test)", phases: ["probe"] };

const inputs = args ?? {};
const STORM_MD5 = "b4aefd29108f232f9c0d5a4b030215c1";

const tasks = [
  { key: "vompl-1", label: "vompl-1 utc time", cmd: "date -u" },
  { key: "vompl-2", label: "vompl-2 proc count", cmd: "ls /proc | grep -E '^[0-9]+$' | wc -l" },
  { key: "vompl-3", label: "vompl-3 hostname", cmd: "hostname" },
  { key: "vompl-4", label: "vompl-4 hosts lines", cmd: "wc -l < /etc/hosts" },
  { key: "vompl-5", label: "vompl-5 tmp count", cmd: "ls /tmp | wc -l" },
];

const schema = {
  type: "object",
  required: ["task", "body", "body_md5", "body_len"],
  properties: {
    task: { type: "string" },
    body: { type: "string" },
    body_md5: { type: "string" },
    body_len: { type: "integer" }
  }
};

const results = [];
for (const t of tasks) {
  phase("probe");
  const r = agent(
    "Run exactly this shell command and nothing else: " + t.cmd + ". Capture its stdout as BODY (strip trailing newlines). Compute MD5 of BODY bytes with md5sum and byte length with wc -c. Reply with ONLY this JSON envelope, no other text: {\"status\": \"ok\", \"result\": {\"task\": \"" + t.label + "\", \"body\": BODY, \"body_md5\": MD5, \"body_len\": LEN}}",
    { key: t.key, label: t.label, schema: schema, timeoutMs: 120000 }
  );
  const genuine = r.body_md5 !== STORM_MD5 && r.body_len > 0;
  results.push({ task: r.task, body: r.body, body_md5: r.body_md5, genuine: genuine });
  log(t.key + " genuine=" + genuine + " md5=" + r.body_md5);
}

const genuineCount = results.filter(function (x) { return x.genuine; }).length;
return {
  message: "vompl-5 complete: " + genuineCount + "/5 genuine",
  genuine: genuineCount,
  total: 5,
  results: results
};
```

Wait — the prompt concatenation with t.label inside JSON: labels contain spaces, fine. But careful: the child's BODY might contain quotes/newlines that break the JSON envelope the child returns. The child is an agent; instruct it to produce valid JSON (escape as needed). Agents usually handle this. To be safer, I could ask the child to base64 the body... but then I can't easily compare md5... actually I CAN: md5 of base64-decoded... no, workflow JS can't decode base64 (no Buffer/Node APIs — actually atob? Not listed; only the listed globals. Risky). Keep raw body; these trivial outputs are single-line alnum. Fine.

Also `phase("probe")` inside loop — phase() marks progress; calling repeatedly is fine.

One more consideration: sequential `agent()` calls — the runtime executes top-level statements; `agent()` presumably blocks until the child returns (it's described as "asks the child agent to return..."). The earlier successful runs used workflow child agents ~1-2s each. Good.

Timeout: 120000ms each.

Now — the todo list retry, then workflow.create, then workflow.launch (synchronous? 5×~2s = ~10-15s plus overhead; launch (blocking) is fine, or launch_async + view_run. I'll use launch and wait — simpler, bounded).

Actually the acceptance test says run them "one after another" — sequential in the script. Good.

Let me also think: does creating/launching this from the dirty main chat poison the workflow children? Earlier evidence: workflow child agents ran clean (5/5 twice, including once launched at 06:35Z and 07:55Z). The workflow run itself is daemon-executed with its own session. Should be clean.

Write the todo first (now with correct schema), then create the workflow. These are dependent (todo is just bookkeeping) — do todo.write + workflow.create in the same block? The create doesn't depend on the todo. Yes, batch them.

