ore returning to workflow JavaScript. Without `options.schema`, `status: \"ok\"` returns a plain result string. Use `options.schema` whenever workflow JavaScript needs to read fields from the result; the schema describes the unwrapped `result` object only, and schema results are returned as validated JSON. Every workflow schema is a minimum contract: required fields and all declared constraints remain enforced while additional object properties are accepted recursively. Declare every field that workflow JavaScript reads. Give every child call a stable unique `key`, set `label` for readable activity, pass `phase` when spawning inside fan-out, and use longer `timeoutMs` values for research or production children.\n\n`parallel(thunks, { concurrency })` runs independent work concurrently and returns an array in input order. A non-fatal item failure returns `null` for that item. `pipeline(items, stage1, stage2, ...)` runs every item through the stages independently, with no barrier between stages; each stage receives `(prevResult, originalItem, index)`, and a non-fatal item failure returns `null` for that item. Filter nulls explicitly and use `log()` for dropped or bounded coverage.\n\nDefault to `pipeline()` for per-item multi-stage work. Use a barrier with `parallel()` only when the next step genuinely needs the whole previous result set together, such as dedupe, merge, full-set synthesis, or early exit.\n\n### Working directory\n\nEach workflow execution provides one pre-created, shared working directory under the hidden workflow run directory. Exec commands start there unless the child specifies another workdir. Put helper scripts, raw drafts, downloaded inputs, intermediate notes, source files, generated media, renders, verifier files, and other temporary output there. This overrides skill or template defaults that put `.src/`, media, source, build, or validation output beside a final deliverable. A final `workspace/your_files/<short-slug>/` directory should contain